llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
129): a gfx803 flash-attention kernel for prompt-sized batches at head sizes
96 and 128.
gfx803-specific: engages only for cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4 (host) and GCN4
(device code). launch_fattn() gains a parameter whose default keeps every
other kernel's behaviour.

WHY
---
Prompt attention grows with the square of the prompt. At head size 128 the
tile kernel runs it at 2.0-2.5 TFLOPS where it serves the query heads of one
K/V head together, and at 0.9 where the GQA ratio is odd and it cannot
(patch 128's one-head tiles). Qwen2.5-Coder 7B has 28 query heads over 4 K/V
heads: a 13,316-token prompt spends 42.6 of its 97.3 s of GPU time in
flash_attn_tile<128, 128, 16, 1>. At head size 96 the grouped tiles are
10-20x slower still.

Patch 129's kernel reaches 3.5 TFLOPS but keeps 128 query rows of Q in
shared memory as fp32: 128 x 132 x 4 bytes = 67.6 KB at head size 128, more
than the 64 KiB of a CU before the K/V tile.

WHAT
----
fattn-gcn4-prompt.cuh adds flash_attn_gcn4_prompt_qreg<D, R> for D = 96 and
128, patch 129's layout with Q in registers:

  - The eight lanes of a 16-lane row that share a set of query rows hold
    those rows' Q split into eight d-slices. Q*K^T runs in eight steps: at
    step s a lane takes slice (ty+s)%8 from the lane 2s further along
    through the DPP row rotation of the multiply-add, as P*V already does for
    the softmax weights, and reads the matching slice of its keys from
    shared memory. Reading Q costs no instruction, and only K and V occupy
    shared memory (33 KB at head size 128).
  - Each lane owns R query rows and 4 keys of its wave's score tile: blocks of
    64 (R = 2, 197 VGPRs) or 96 query rows (R = 3, 242 VGPRs), no spills. A
    96-row block takes about a fifth less time per row but pads a batch
    further; the host picks the one with fewer query rows per CU over whole
    waves of blocks, within 2.9% of the faster one on geometric mean over the
    sweep below. 128-row blocks spill.

launch_fattn() splits a head's keys over several blocks (parallel_blocks)
whenever that fills the last wave of blocks better. For this kernel the
partial results and flash_attn_combine_results cost more than that tail:
4 K/V heads, GQA 4, 2048 queries over 512 keys take 2.8 ms with one block per
output tile and 9.4 ms with two, and 512 queries over 512 keys 0.73 against
0.94-1.10 ms. Its new split_kv_for_tail parameter (default true) lets a
kernel ask for a split only where one block per output tile would leave more
than a quarter of the dispatched block slots idle, the threshold llama.cpp
already uses to choose stream-k. Why the partial-result stores of this kernel
are that slow is not known: wider stores, a split-major layout, half as many
writers and writing into the output tensor itself left it unchanged, and
with the writes removed the split costs only the combine kernel.

The kernel takes a batch from 24 query rows where the tile kernel would serve
one query head per K/V head; otherwise from 64 query rows at head size 128
and from 8 at head size 96. Conditions as in patch 129: no ALiBi, no logit
softcap, no sinks, equal K and V head sizes, 16-byte aligned Q rows, any K/V
type the tile kernel takes.

Measured on an RX 590, test-backend-ops perf, f16 K/V, mask, 4 K/V heads,
GQA 1/2/4/7/8, 64-2048 queries, 512-16384 keys (180 shapes): geometric mean
0.485x the time of the shipped kernels. At model shapes, ms per call:

                                   keys   queries   before   after    TFLOPS
    Qwen3 8B (8 K/V heads, GQA 4)   4096    512      13.8     10.5   2.5 -> 3.3
                                   16384    512      67.5     41.2   2.0 -> 3.3
                                   16384   2048     261.8    142.1   2.1 -> 3.9
    Coder 7B (4 K/V heads, GQA 7)   4096    512      34.2      8.7   0.9 -> 3.5
                                   16384    512     137.3     34.5   0.9 -> 3.5
                                   16384   2048     549.2    126.8   0.9 -> 3.8

Result on an RX 590, llama-server, 16k context, q8_0 KV cache, prompt time:

    Qwen2.5-Coder 7B Q4_K_M, 13,316 tokens   97.4 -> 65.8 s
                              4,625 tokens   24.4 -> 20.3 s
    Qwen3 8B Q4_K_M,         13,299 tokens   78.3 -> 74.6 s
                              4,608 tokens   22.3 -> 21.7 s

Through Ollama (same prompts, the image's defaults): Coder 7B 90.3 -> 56.5 s,
Qwen3 8B 78.0 -> 73.0 s for the 13k-token prompt.

rocprof: Coder 7B's attention 42.6 -> 10.7 s, Qwen3 8B's 21.1 -> 17.1 s
(512-token batches over 32 heads leave part of the last wave of blocks idle,
so there the kernel runs at about 3.0 TFLOPS). Generation and the greedy
answers are unchanged.

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT
passes all 3982 cases against the CPU backend, plus 938 added cases at head
sizes 96 and 128 (GQA 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, two sequences, 32-512 queries around
the 64- and 96-row block edges, 113 / 512 / 1000 keys, with and without a
mask, and q8_0 K/V at 4096 and 8192 keys) and patch 129's 1212. On the cases
the new kernel serves the error against the CPU is at parity with the tile
kernel (geometric mean 1.025x). To reproduce, build llama.cpp b11232 with
GGML_HIP=ON and AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803, then run with and without the patch:

    test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT
    llama-server -m <Qwen2.5-Coder 7B Q4_K_M> -c 16384 -ngl 99 -fa on
        -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0
        (send a 13,000-token prompt; the response's timings give the
        prompt time)

